WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.263
"Should We Be Concerned About
Ochratoxin and Aflatoxin?"

00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:13.520
Ochratoxin has been described
as toxic to the immune system,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.360
developing fetus, kidneys,
nervous system, as well as

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.520
being carcinogenic—but
that’s in animal studies.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.760
Ochratoxin causes kidney
toxicity in certain animal species,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.880
but there is little documented
evidence of adverse effects in humans.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.640
That’s why it’s only considered
a possible human carcinogen.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:35.000
Big Ag assures that current
ochratoxin levels are safe

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.960
even among those who eat a
lot of contaminated foods.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.800
The worst-case scenario may
be young children eating a lot

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.880
of oat-based cereals, but even then,
their lifetime cancer risk

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.120
is considered negligible,
with those arguing against

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.800
regulatory standards suggesting
you can eat more than

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.760
42 cups of oatmeal a day
and not worry about it.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.080
Where do they get these
kinds of estimates?

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.720
They determine the so-called
benchmark dose in animals—

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.200
the dose of the toxin that
gives a 10% increase in pathology—

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.440
and then, because you want
to err on the side of caution,

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.560
you divide that dose by 500
as a kind of safety fudge factor

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.760
to develop the tolerable daily intake.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.400
For cancer risk, you
can find the tumor dose—

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.160
the dose that increases
tumor incidence in lab animals

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.280
by 5%—and extrapolate down to
the ”negligible cancer risk intake,”

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.560
effectively incorporating
a 5,000-fold safety factor.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.240
It seems kind of arbitrary, right?
But what else are you going to do?

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.480
You can’t just intentionally feed people
the stuff and see what happens.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.600
Though look, people eat
it all the time.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.120
Can’t we just follow people and
their diets out over time

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.800
and see if people who eat more
whole grains like oats, for example,

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.240
are more likely to have
cancer or live shorter lives?

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.760
What is the association
between whole grain intake

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.560
and all-cause, cardiovascular,
and cancer mortality?

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.200
Every additional ounce of whole
grains a day is associated with

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.560
not only a lower risk for
cancer but also a lower risk

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.200
of dying from all
causes put together.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.560
Here are all the big cancer studies.
Every single one, if anything,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.160
trended towards lower cancer risk.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.960
Bottom line is that you
don’t find adverse effects

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.520
confirmed in these population studies.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.480
This is not to say ochratoxin
is necessarily harmless,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.880
but any such risk it
does pose doesn’t outweigh

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.520
the known beneﬁts of
whole grain consumption.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.240
In fact, healthy constituents of
the whole grains themselves

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.240
like the antioxidants may
directly reduce the impacts

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.200
of mycotoxins by protecting
cells from damage,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.360
so eating lots of fruits and
vegetables may also help.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.400
Either way, a healthy diet
can play a signiﬁcant role

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.320
in mitigating the risk.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.640
In summary, healthy foods
like whole grains are good,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.440
but just not as good as they
could be because of ochratoxin;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.040
whereas less healthful foods,
like wine and pork,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.240
are worse because of the mycotoxin.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.120
Ochratoxin was detected, for example,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.000
in 44% of tested pork.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.080
In recent years, mold has
been blamed for all sorts

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.360
of vague subjective symptoms, but
we have little scientific evidence

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.960
that mold has anything
to do with them.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.200
However, this concept of
“toxic mold syndrome” has permeated

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.960
the public consciousness, perpetuated
by disreputable predatory practices,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.520
by those making money testing
homes for mold spores or

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.280
testing people’s urine or blood.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.560
But all these tests are said to just
further propagate misinformation

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.360
and inflict unnecessary,
and often exorbitant costs

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.040
on patients desperate for a diagnosis.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.480
“The continued belief in this myth
is perpetuated by those charlatans

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.840
who believe that measles vaccines
cause autism, that homeopathy works,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.240
that fluoride in the water
should be removed . . .”

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.360
Mold toxin contamination of
food, however, has emerged

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.440
as a legitimate issue
of serious concern,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.360
perhaps even more important
than other contaminants

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.800
that might make their way
into the food supply.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.200
Hundreds of different types have
been identified, but only one has been

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.440
classified as a known human
carcinogen, and that’s aflatoxin.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.480
The ochratoxin I talked about before
is a possible human carcinogen,

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.840
but we know aflatoxin causes
cancer in human beings.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.200
In fact, aﬂatoxins are amongst
the most powerful

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.080
carcinogens we know about.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.560
For example, it has been
estimated that about a fifth of all

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.320
liver cancer cases may be
attributable to aflatoxins,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.720
and since liver cancer is
the third-leading cause of

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.600
cancer deaths worldwide, and
kills so rapidly after diagnosis,

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.720
the contribution of aflatoxins to
this deadly cancer is quite significant.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.240
And once it makes it into
the food, there is almost nothing

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.800
you can do to remove it.
Cooking, for example, doesn’t help.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.040
So, once it makes it into crops,
or the meat, dairy, and eggs

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.960
from animals consuming
those crops, it’s too late.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.120
So, we have to prevent contamination
in the first place,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.920
and that’s what we’ve been doing
for decades in this country.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.640
Because of government regulations,
companies are almost

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.840
“always sampling” for aflatoxin,
resulting in nearly a billion dollars

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.840
in losses every year, which may
get worse if climate change worsens

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.200
aflatoxin contamination
in the Midwest Corn Belt.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.480
So, on a consumer level it’s really
more of a public health problem

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.920
in the less industrialized
world such as African countries

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.320
where conditions are ripe,
and farmers can’t afford

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.160
to throw away a billion
dollars’ worth of crops.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.360
Indeed, aflatoxin remains a
public health threat in Africa,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.480
rural China, and Southeast Asia,
affecting more than half of humanity,

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.080
which explains why the prevalence
of liver cancer in those areas

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.760
may be 30 times higher, but it is not
a major problem in the U.S. or Europe.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.240
For example, only about 1% of
Americans have detectible levels

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.680
of aflatoxins in their bloodstream.
Why even 1%, though?

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.400
Well, the FDA works to ensure
that the levels of exposure

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.360
to these toxins are not
kept as low as possible,

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.080
but, instead, as low as practical.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.960
For example, in California there
has been an increase in unacceptable

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.840
aflatoxin levels in pistachios,
almonds, and figs.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.160
Unacceptable in Europe, that is,
so it affects our ability to export,

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.960
but not necessarily unacceptable
for U.S. consumers,

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.760
as we allow twice as much
aflatoxin contamination.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.240
Figs are unique since they’re
allowed to dry on the tree.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.640
This makes them particularly
susceptible to aﬂatoxin production.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.600
It would be interesting to know
about the fig consuming habits

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.160
of the 1% of Americans who
turned up positive for the toxin.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.920
If figs were to blame, I’d
encourage people to diversify

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.720
their dried fruit consumption,
but nuts are so good for us

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.560
that we really want to
keep them in our diets.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.680
The cardiovascular health beneﬁts
outweigh the carcinogenic effects,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.960
preventing thousands of
strokes and heart attacks

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.240
for every one case of liver cancer.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.440
“Thus, the population health beneﬁts
provided by increased nut consumption

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:49.040
clearly outweigh the risks associated
with increased aﬂatoxin exposure.”

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.760
So, we’re left with aflatoxin
being mostly a problem

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.800
in the developing world, and
because of that, it remains

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.720
a largely and rather shamefully
ignored global health issue.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.720
Where attention has been paid,
it has been largely driven

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.520
by the need to meet stringent import
regulations in the richer nations

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.640
of the world rather than to
protect the billions of people

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.403
exposed on a daily basis.

